" (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v30i2p8/pdf/)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 28; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"pattern\":\"Conjecture 42\"}); Total lines: 1168\nNo matching text found for \"Conjecture 42\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v30i2p8/pdf/)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 28; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"pattern\":\"weak order\"}); Total lines: 1168\nL936@P21: Thus the vectors {qC : C ∈ A} are linearly dependent, so A is dependent in δOW (Q).\nL937@P21: This shows that for matroids such that δM = (C, A0), the combinatorial derived\nL938@P21: matroid is generic, in a precise sense. In particular, this applies to uniform matroids\nL939@P21: U(k, n) with k > n − 3, by Corollary 27. We also expect this to apply to many other\nL940@P21: naturally occurring matroids of high rank. The simple Lemma 40 also shows that if\nL941@P21: δM = (C, A0) we can characterize representations for which the Oxley–Wang derived\nL942@P21: matroid equals the combinatorial derived matroid from mere enumerative properties of\nL943@P21: δOW (Q).\nL944@P21: Corollary 41. If δM = (C, A0) and Q is a representation of M such that δOW (Q) has\nL945@P21: the same number of dependent sets as δM, then δOW (Q) = δM.\nL946@P21: Proof. By Lemma 40 we have D(δM) = A0 ⊆ D(δOW (Q)), so since the two sets are finite,\nL947@P21: they must be equal if they have the same cardinality. Since δM and δOW (Q) also have\nL948@P21: the same ground set C, they must thus be equal.\nL949@P21: In general, if M is representable, we conjecture that the combinatorial derived matroid\nL950@P21: is generic in two different ways; one categorical and one algebraic-geometric. In order to\nL951@P21: state Conjectures 43 and 45, we need some auxiliary standard definitions.\nL952@P21-22: the electronic journal of combinatorics 30(2) (2023), #P2.8 22Definition 42. Let E be a finite set. The weak order on the class M of matroids with\nL953@P22: ground set E is the partial order with M1 > M2 if every dependent set in M1 is also\nL954@P22: dependent in M2.\nL955@P22: Conjecture 43. Let M be a representable matroid with set of circuits C. Then δM >\nL956@P22: δOW (R) for every representation R of M, where the order relation is the weak order on\nL957@P22: the set of matroids on C.\nL958@P22: Note that Lemma 40 proves Conjecture 43 in the case that δM = (C, A0). It is\nL959@P22: conceivable that Conjecture 43 may be approached with techniques similar to those in [9].\nL960@P22: There, the authors studied conditions under which the collection of matroids with some\nL961@P22: circuits prescribed have a maximal element in the weak order.\nL962@P22: The next example shows that it is possible that M is representable over a field F,\nL963@P22: without any of the representations of M over F (or indeed over any extension field of F)\nL964@P22: yielding δM as an Oxley-Wang derived matroid.\nL965@P22: Example 44. Consider the graphical matroid M = M(K4) as in Example 28. Recall\nL966@P22: that δM is the non-Fano matroid F\nL967@P22: −\nL968@P22: 7\nL969@P22: , with dependent sets A = A0.\nL970@P22: We will now look at Oxley–Wang derived matroids of M(K4). By Lemma 40, all sets\nL971@P22: in A = A0 are dependent in δOW (R) for any representation over any field. Moreover, a\nL972@P22: triple of circuit vectors can only be linearly dependent if every element in E occurs in at\nL973@P22: least two of the circuits, which follows from Lemma 34. The only possible dependent set\nL974@P22: in δOW (R), except for the sets in A, is therefore the triple of 4-circuits {1346, 1256, 2345}.\nL975@P22: It is straightforward to see that these circuits are linearly dependent in the (projectively\nL976@P22: unique) representation of M over a field F or characteristic 2, but not in a representation\nL977@P22: over fields of characteristic 6= 2. This can intuitively be understood if we interpret a linear\nL978@P22: combination of the circuit vectors supported on the circuits {1346, 1256, 2345} as a formal\nL979@P22: sum of the cycles {abcd, abdc, acbd} in the graph. In order for such a formal sum to take\nL980@P22: the value zero on the edge 6 = cd, the coefficient in front of the (directed) cycles abcd and\nL981@P22: abdc must be the same, say α. But then the formal sum of cycles traverses the edge ab\nL982@P22: 2α times in the same direction, and so it can only be zero if 2α = 0, i.e., if char(F) = 2.\nL983@P22: Thus, we see that δOW (R) is the Fano matroid in characteristic 2, and the non-Fano\nL984@P22: matroid in characteristic 6= 2. In particular, we get δ(M) = δOW (R) for a representation\nL985@P22: R of M if and only if the representation is in characteristic 6= 2.\nL986@P22: For algebraic-geometric notions of genericity, fix a field F and let F be its algebraic\nL987@P22: closure. Assume that M is representable over F, with |E(M)| = n and r(M) = k. We\nL988@P22: can then define the variety V (M) = VF(M) ⊆ F\nL989@P22: k×n\nL990@P22: of matrices that represent M. This\nL991@P22: is clearly an algebraic variety, since it is defined by the vanishing of certain finitely many\nL992@P22: minors and the non-vanishing of finitely many others. We also have a variety V (N) ⊆\nL993@P22: F\nL994@P22: (n−k)×|C| for every possible F-representable matroid N on the ground set C = C(M).\nL995@P22: Then the map\nL996@P22: R 7→ δOW (R)\nL997@P22-23: the electronic journal of combinatorics 30(2) (2023), #P2.8 23subdivides V (M) into components\nL998@P23: VN (M) = {R ∈ V (M) : δOW (R) = N},\nL999@P23: for different possible Oxley–Wang derived matroids N of M. From this perspective,\nL1000@P23: genericity is captured by the following conjecture.\nL1001@P23: Conjecture 45. For every matroid N 6= δM on C, if VN (M) is non-empty, then it has\nL1002@P23: positive codimension in V (M).\nL1003@P23: Note that it is possible that VN (M) is empty over certain ground fields F. Example\nL1004@P23: 5.5 shows that this is true, in particular, when M = M(K4) and F is the two element\nL1005@P23: field.\nL1006@P23: Since there are only finitely many possible Oxley–Wang derived matroids, this would\nL1007@P23: imply that VδM(M) would be a full-dimensional subvariety of V (M). In particular, assume\nL1008@P23: that F is a finite field and that F\nL1009@P23: 0/F is a finite field extension whose degree [F0\nL1010@P23: : F] tends to\nL1011@P23: infinity. Conjecture 45 would then imply that asymptotically almost all representations\nL1012@P23: R of M over F\nL1013@P23: 0 would satisfy δOW (R) = δM.\nL1014@P23: 8.2 Adjoint\nL1015@P23: The construction of the combinatorial derived matroid is iterative. If a set of circuits of\nL1016@P23: M satisfies the nullity condition (3) in Definition 13, the set is in A0 hence dependent. If\nL1017@P23: not, the only way to check its (in)dependence is by constructing iteratively all dependent\nL1018@P23: sets of δM and see if our set is one of them.\nL1019@P23: The fact that there are sets for which (in)dependence is hard to check, means it is\nL1020@P23: also difficult to define a closure function of δM. We are not optimistic about finding a\nL1021@P23: definition of closure in δM without some form of iteration.\nL1022@P23: As a result of the lack of closure in δM, we have not managed to compare the definition\nL1023@P23: of δM, that uses dependent sets, to that of the adjoint (Section 3.4), that uses closed sets.\nL1024@P23: We expect the following to be true.\nL1025@P23: Conjecture 46. If an adjoint of a matroid M exists, one of them is equal to the com\u0002binatorial derived matroid of the dual. Moreover, every adjoint of M is less δM∗\nL1026@P23: in the\nL1027@P23: weak order on matroids with ground set C.\nL1028@P23: 9 Suggestions for further research\nL1029@P23: We have developed the notion of the combinatorial derived matroid. Contrary to earlier\nL1030@P23: constructions for a derived matroid, our construction exists for all matroids and is unique.\nL1031@P23: There are many open questions about our construction. Apart from the questions and\nL1032@P23: conjectures already stated in the text, we list here some more ideas for further research.\nL1033@P23: Question 47. Is there a characterization of the class of matroids N that are isomorphic\nL1034@P23: to the combinatorial derived matroid δM of some matroid M?\nL1035@P23-24: the electronic journal of combinatorics 30(2) (2023), #P2.8 24By Proposition 4.12, a necessary condition is that every element in N that is contained\nL1036@P24: in a circuit is also contained in a circuit of size 3. Many more necessary conditions like\nL1037@P24: this should probably first be obtained, before a complete characterization is feasible.\nL1038@P24: Question 48. For which matroids does it hold that δM = M or δM = M∗? The latter\nL1039@P24: property may be more natural, since the rank of δM is bounded from above by the rank\nL1040@P24: of M∗. It is also possible to repeat the procedure of taking the combinatorial derived\nL1041@P24: matroid of the dual multiple times, i.e. study the sequence\nL1042@P24: M, δ(M∗), δ(δ(M∗)\nL1043@P24: ∗\nL1044@P24: ), . . . .\nL1045@P24: Does this sequence converge? For earlier constructions of the derived matroid, these\nL1046@P24: questions have been studied in [15, Section 3] and [10, Section 4], see also [11].\nL1047@P24: Question 49. Is there any relation between δM and δM∗?\nL1048@P24: Question 50. There are matroids that do not have an adjoint, whereas the combinatorial\nL1049@P24: derived matroid always exists. This means one of the properties in Definition 9 and/or\nL1050@P24: Lemma 10 is not in general satisfied by the combinatorial derived matroid. Which one?\nL1051@P24: Question 51. If X is a representation of M by topological spheres, as in [17, 2], does X\nL1052@P24: induce a representation δX of δM by topological spheres?\nL1053@P24: Acknowledgements\nL1054@P24: The authors would like to thank the anonymous reviewer for their careful reading of\nL1055@P24: the manuscript and many useful suggestions for improvement. Furthermore we thank\nL1056@P24: Santiago Guzm´an-Pro for useful discussions, and in particular for providing Example 36,\nL1057@P24: a counterexample to a statement in an earlier version of this paper.\nL1058@P24: References\nL1059@P24: [1] Marion Alfter, Walter Kern, and Alfred Wanka. On adjoints and dual matroids.\nL1060@P24: Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B, 50:208–213, 1990.\nL1061@P24: [2] Laura Anderson. Homotopy sphere representations for matroids. Annals of Combi\u0002natorics, 16(2):189–202, 2012.\nL1062@P24: [3] Alan L Cheung. Adjoints of a geometry. Canadian Mathematical Bulletin, 17(3):363–\nL1063@P24: 365, 1974.\nL1064@P24: [4] Collette Coullard and Lisa Hellerstein. Independence and port oracles for matroids,\nL1065@P24: with an application to computational learning theory. Combinatorica, 16, 02 1998.\nL1066@P24: [5] Henry Crapo. An algebra of pieces of space — Hermann Grassmann to Gian\nL1067@P24: Carlo Rota. In Ernesto Damiani, Ottavio D’Antona, Vincenzo Marra, and Fabrizio\nL1068@P24: Palombi, editors, From Combinatorics to Philosophy, pages 61–90, Boston, MA, 2009.\nL1069@P24: Springer US.\nL1070@P24-25: the electronic journal of combinatorics 30(2) (2023), #P2.8 25[6] Henry Crapo and William Schmitt. The Whitney algebra of a matroid. Journal of\nL1071@P25: Combinatorial Theory, Series A, 91:215–263, 2000.\nL1072@P25: [7] Ragnar Freij-Hollanti and Olga Kuznetsova. Information hiding using matroid theory.\nL1073@P25: Advances in Applied Mathematics, 129:102205, 18, 2021.\nL1074@P25: [8] Santiago Guzm´an-Pro. Personal communication, 2022.\nL1075@P25: [9] Bill Jackson and Shin-ichi Tanigawa. Maximal matroids in weak order posets.\nL